title: Meridian Mk II — an open field weather station
author: Babb Works

This page is one document that four different people read differently. A
visitor sees the project. A contributor also sees the build. Procurement
also sees what things cost. Compliance also sees the test results.

Nothing is duplicated and nothing is hidden by a second copy. The page
says what each part requires, and the site showing it decides who gets
what. If a site cannot decide, it shows the sentence the author wrote for
exactly that case.

---

## What it is

Meridian is a solar-powered weather station meant to sit on a fence post
for five years without anyone visiting it. Temperature, humidity,
pressure, wind, and rainfall, logged locally and pushed over LoRa when a
gateway is in range.

The Mk II is the first version we would put somewhere inconvenient.

## Specifications
id: specs
prefer-layout: table, list
source: https://meridian.example/specs.json

A site that can fetch and lay out a table shows the current figures. This
sentence is what everyone else gets: enclosure 180×140×95mm, IP67, mast
or wall mount, 2.4W panel, 18650 cell, LoRa 915MHz, sampling every 60
seconds, five-year design life.

## Field photographs
id: field-photos
type: gallery
source: https://meridian.example/photos
prefer-layout: grid, carousel, list

Eleven photographs from the Skeena valley deployment, September to March,
including two weeks under snow. A site that implements galleries shows
them; this sentence is the caption for one that does not.

